Sequence of events is as follows:
1. Mis-key wield command.  Instead of wielding uncursed mattock wield
dagger which turns out to be cursed.
2. a cursed dagger (being wielded).
3. fortunately, I'm in a shop, so I buy a remove curse scroll from the
shopkeeper. Read it .. .
- you feel like someone is helping you -
- the scroll disintegrates! -

Check - still wielding a "cursed dagger".  Nope, can't unwield it.

I have NOT source dived yet,  but I consider two possibilities:
1) IF the "remove curse" has a chance of failure, then clearly it
failed.  A comparison of spell success rate vs. RNG = "saving throw".

The concept of "saving throw" exists in nethack even if we do not use
that name for it.  When a monster with a magic resistance probability
succesfully resists magic, it has made a "saving throw".  When I
#untrap a chest, trigger the trap and get the message "but luckily, the
gas blows away!" , I have made a saving throw. When a cockatrice
touches me and I don't start turning to stone, I have made  a saving
throw.

A saving throw is nothing but a comparison of a random number vs. some
set chance that I or a monster will not suffer the full effects of a
malicious effect.  It is very similar to the idea of "magic
cancellation".  It is correct general gaming terminology.

2) Now that you've spoken up, you're beginning to give me the idea that
there is NOT a probability of failure for "remove curse". In which case
the other option is that it is caused by a cursed scroll -- in which
case something in my inventory has been cursed, I would think.

Since you and Seraphim are positive that this is the case, I will
accept that as true until I have had a chance to source-dive for
myself. 

